<!-- Golden case F: the P0 cross-patient/cross-invoice boundary bug, fixed.

     Two invoices, two different `<abrechnungsfall>` — two different patients. Invoice 1 charges
     GOÄ 34 alone; invoice 2 charges GOÄ 4 alone. Nothing else is on either invoice.

     excl_auto_34_4 (data/rules/exclusions.csv, verified=true, direction=one_way): if GOÄ 34 is
     charged, GOÄ 4 is not chargeable beside it. Before the fix, `audit_delivery` flattened every
     invoice's positions into one Ziffer-keyed fact set before grounding the rules engine, so this
     rule fired the moment GOÄ 34 and GOÄ 4 both appeared *anywhere* in the delivery — even though
     they belong to two different patients who never claimed both. Invoice 2's GOÄ 4 was reported
     `blocked` by GOÄ 34 and moved to `confirmed_wrong`, which is wrong under any reading of the
     GOÄ: two patients cannot render a service "alongside" each other.

     After the fix, `audit_delivery` runs Soufflé once per `<abrechnungsfall>`. Neither invoice's
     own fact set ever contains both Ziffern, so the exclusion cannot fire on either side — GOÄ 34
     and GOÄ 4 are each `chargeable`, and `confirmed_wrong_eur` is 0.00.

     `verified_rule_ids` is empty on both positions rather than `["excl_auto_34_4"]`. That is not a
     regression: `rules_bearing_on` credits a verified rule to an invoice only when *every* Ziffer
     it names is claimed there (see `test_case_a_every_position_is_chargeable_and_unconfirmed` for
     the same rule applied to case A) — a lone GOÄ 34 with no GOÄ 4 on the same abrechnungsfall was
     never actually tested against this exclusion, so crediting it would overclaim coverage the same
     way the original bug overclaimed a defect. Both positions land in `unconfirmed`, honestly:
     nothing confirms them wrong, and nothing verified confirms them clean either.

     Synthetic data throughout: echtdaten="0". -->
